.TH ATLAS-READELF-OBJDUMP 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-readelf\-objdump \- Read a Linux program without running it: headers, symbols, sections, protections and disassembly.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
readelf  \-h  hello           # ELF header: class (32/64\-bit), machine, entry point
readelf  \-s  hello           # symbol table: function and variable names
readelf  \-lW hello           # program headers: look for GNU_STACK (executable stack?)
objdump  \-d \-M intel hello   # disassemble code, Intel syntax
objdump  \-s \-j .rodata hello # hex dump of one section
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
First look at a binary challenge: architecture, symbols, protections.
.IP \(bu 2
Find the function that checks the password and read its assembly.
.IP \(bu 2
See which strings and constants are stored in the file.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fBreadelf \-h\fR
ELF header.
.br
Use it when: 32\-bit or 64\-bit? which CPU?
.TP
\fBreadelf \-S / \-lW\fR
Section headers / program headers (wide).
.br
Use it when: Stack flags, section layout.
.TP
\fBreadelf \-s\fR
Symbols.
.br
Use it when: Function names in a non\-stripped binary.
.TP
\fBreadelf \-p .rodata\fR
Print a section as strings.
.br
Use it when: Constants and messages.
.TP
\fBobjdump \-d\fR
Disassemble executable sections.
.br
Use it when: Read the logic.
.TP
\fB\-M intel\fR
Intel syntax instead of AT&T.
.br
Use it when: Easier to read, matches most tutorials.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-show\-raw\-insn\fR
Hide the machine\-code bytes.
.br
Use it when: Shorter output.
.TP
\fBobjdump \-s \-j SECTION\fR
Hex dump of a section.
.br
Use it when: Data tables, embedded text.
.TP
\fBnm / file / strings\fR
Related quick checks.
.br
Use it when: Symbols, type, text.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. What kind of program is it?"
The ELF header tells you the word size, CPU, and whether the program is position independent.
.PP
.nf
$ readelf \-h hello | grep \-E 'Class|Machine|Type|Entry'
.fi
.PP
.nf
  Class:                             ELF64
  Type:                              DYN (Position\-Independent Executable file)
  Machine:                           Advanced Micro Devices X86\-64
  Entry point address:               0x1080
.fi
.PP
64\-bit x86\-64 and a position\-independent executable (type \fBDYN\fR). This decides which debugger setup and which addresses to expect.
.SS "2. Which functions does it have or use?"
Symbols list what the program defines (\fBmain\fR) and imports from libc (\fBputs\fR, \fBstrcmp\fR).
.PP
.nf
$ readelf \-s hello | grep \-E ' main$| puts| strcmp'
.fi
.PP
.nf
     3: 0000000000000000     0 FUNC    GLOBAL DEFAULT  UND puts@GLIBC_2.2.5 (3)
    21: 0000000000000000     0 FUNC    GLOBAL DEFAULT  UND puts@GLIBC_2.2.5
    25: 0000000000000000     0 FUNC    GLOBAL DEFAULT  UND strcmp@GLIBC_2.2.5
    32: 0000000000001169   110 FUNC    GLOBAL DEFAULT   16 main
.fi
.PP
Seeing \fBstrcmp\fR hints at a password comparison. A stripped binary has no names, so this shows less.
.SS "3. Is the stack executable?"
\fBGNU_STACK\fR flags \fBRW\fR mean a non\-executable stack (NX). \fBRWE\fR would allow shellcode on the stack, as in Narnia.
.PP
.nf
$ readelf \-lW hello | grep \-E 'GNU_STACK|GNU_RELRO'
.fi
.PP
.nf
  GNU_STACK      0x000000 0x0000000000000000 0x0000000000000000 0x000000 0x000000 RW  0x10
  GNU_RELRO      0x002db0 0x0000000000003db0 0x0000000000003db0 0x000250 0x000250 R   0x1
.fi
.PP
The stack segment is read\-write only, which is the modern default. Narnia's binaries differ.
.SS "4. Read the strings the program stores"
\fB.rodata\fR holds constants such as string literals.
.PP
.nf
$ readelf \-p .rodata hello
.fi
.PP
.nf

String dump of section '.rodata':
  [     4]  flag{demo_not_a_real_flag}
  [    1f]  open\-sesame
  [    2b]  access denied
.fi
.PP
The secret passphrase, the denied message and the flag text, all visible without running anything.
.SS "5. Disassemble main"
\fB\-d\fR disassembles; \fBsed \-n '/start/,/end/p'\fR prints just one function.
.PP
.nf
$ objdump \-d \-M intel \-\-no\-show\-raw\-insn hello | sed \-n '/<main>:/,/ret/p'
.fi
.PP
.nf
0000000000001169 <main>:
    1169:	endbr64
    116d:	push   rbp
    116e:	mov    rbp,rsp
    1171:	sub    rsp,0x20
    1175:	mov    DWORD PTR [rbp\-0x14],edi
    1178:	mov    QWORD PTR [rbp\-0x20],rsi
    117c:	lea    rax,[rip+0xe81]        # 2004 <_IO_stdin_used+0x4>
    1183:	mov    QWORD PTR [rbp\-0x8],rax
    1187:	cmp    DWORD PTR [rbp\-0x14],0x1
    118b:	jle    11c1 <main+0x58>
    118d:	mov    rax,QWORD PTR [rbp\-0x20]
    1191:	add    rax,0x8
    1195:	mov    rax,QWORD PTR [rax]
    1198:	lea    rdx,[rip+0xe80]        # 201f <_IO_stdin_used+0x1f>
    119f:	mov    rsi,rdx
    11a2:	mov    rdi,rax
    11a5:	call   1070 <strcmp@plt>
    11aa:	test   eax,eax
    11ac:	jne    11c1 <main+0x58>
    11ae:	mov    rax,QWORD PTR [rbp\-0x8]
    11b2:	mov    rdi,rax
    11b5:	call   1060 <puts@plt>
    11ba:	mov    eax,0x0
    11bf:	jmp    11d5 <main+0x6c>
    11c1:	lea    rax,[rip+0xe63]        # 202b <_IO_stdin_used+0x2b>
    11c8:	mov    rdi,rax
    11cb:	call   1060 <puts@plt>
    11d0:	mov    eax,0x1
    11d5:	leave
.fi
.PP
Look for \fBcmp\fR/\fBcall strcmp\fR followed by a conditional jump (\fBje\fR/\fBjne\fR): that branch decides whether you get the secret. The \fBlea\fR instructions load the string addresses you saw in \fB.rodata\fR.
.SS "6. Hex dump of a section"
\fB\-s\fR prints a section as hex and ASCII, so you can check offsets and hidden bytes.
.PP
.nf
$ objdump \-s \-j .rodata hello
.fi
.PP
.nf

hello:     file format elf64\-x86\-64

Contents of section .rodata:
 2000 01000200 666c6167 7b64656d 6f5f6e6f  ....flag{demo_no
 2010 745f615f 7265616c 5f666c61 677d006f  t_a_real_flag}.o
 2020 70656e2d 73657361 6d650061 63636573  pen\-sesame.acces
 2030 73206465 6e696564 00                 s denied.       
.fi
.PP
The same constants in hex form with their addresses.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Stripped binaries have no function names: you will see \fBsub_...\fR in decompilers and unnamed code here.
.IP \(bu 2
AT&T syntax (the default) puts source before destination; add \fB\-M intel\fR for the order most books use.
.IP \(bu 2
PIE binaries show small addresses until loaded; the real addresses change each run unless ASLR is off.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBobjdump\fR shows what is in the file, not what a packed or obfuscated program does after it unpacks itself.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Find the address of the string \fBaccess denied\fR and the instruction that loads it.
.IP 2. 3
Compile \fBhello.c\fR with \fB\-no\-pie \-fno\-stack\-protector \-z execstack\fR and compare the \fBGNU_STACK\fR line.
.IP 3. 3
Work out which register holds \fBargv[1]\fR at the \fBstrcmp\fR call.
.SH SEE ALSO
ghidra, gdb, radare2 / cutter, ltrace, strace, checksec, nm, strings.
.SH CAPTURE
Output captured 2026\-11\-23 with: GNU readelf (GNU Binutils for Ubuntu) 2.42
